<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jana Gana Mana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11 December <a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/december-11">On this day: 11 December</a> · Also known as জনগণমন</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Jana Gana Mana&quot; is the first of five stanzas of a Bengali hymn, &quot;Bharoto Bhagyo Bidhata&quot;, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first Nobel laureate, completed on 11 December 1911. He wrote it in Sadhu Bhasha, a Sanskritised literary register of Bengali, as a hymn for the monotheistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmo-samaj">Brahmo Samaj</a>, and set it himself in the raga Alhaiya Bilaval, possibly with help from his grand-nephew Dinendranath Tagore.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jana-gana-mana.jpg" alt="Jana Gana Mana" width="330" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rabindranath Tagore · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jana_Gana_Mana_sheet_music.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The song's lyrics first appeared in five stanzas in the Bengali magazine Tattwabodhini Patrika, the official publication of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmo-samaj">Brahmo Samaj</a>, of which Tagore was then editor, under the title Bharat Bhagya Bidhata. Tagore composed the melody himself in the raga Alhaiya Bilaval, possibly with some help from his musician grand-nephew Dinendranath Tagore.</p><p>It was first sung in public on the second day of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> session in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> on 27 December 1911, by Tagore's niece in her school assembly. It was followed in January 1912 at the annual event of the Adi Brahmo Samaj, but remained largely unknown except to readers of the Brahmo journal. On 28 February 1919 Tagore sang it at the Besant Theosophical College in Madanapalle, where Margaret Cousins, the vice-principal, preserved it in Western notation from notes he provided, and where he made his own English translation, The Morning Song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The poem is composed in Sadhu Bhasha, a heavily Sanskritised literary register of Bengali. Its five verses make up a hymn; only the first was adopted as the national anthem. A formal rendition takes about 52 seconds, and a shortened version of the first and last lines takes about 20 seconds.</p><p>Its first stanza names the provinces that were under direct British rule, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a>, Dravida, Utkala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, but none of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>. Defenders hold that Tagore named only border regions in order to include all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> chose the song as a national anthem while in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and on 11 September 1942 the Hamburg Radio Symphony Orchestra played it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s anthem at the founding meeting of the German-Indian Society. The Constituent Assembly closed its midnight session on 14 August 1947 with a unanimous performance, and adopted the first stanza as the National Anthem on 24 January 1950.</p><p>A dispute arose from reports in the British Indian press that a song of welcome to the Emperor had been sung at the 1911 Congress. Many historians hold the reports mistaken, because a different Hindi song, Badshah Humara, praised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> at the same session, and the nationalist press and the official Congress report distinguished the two. Tagore wrote in 1937 and 1939 that the Lord of Destiny of the song could never be George V or any other George. He had renounced a knighthood in 1919 in protest over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jana Gana Mana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Jana Gana Mana</b>&quot; is the first of five stanzas of a Bengali hymn, &quot;Bharoto Bhagyo Bidhata&quot;, which Rabindranath Tagore, India's first Nobel laureate, completed on 11 December 1911.</blockquote><a href="/songs/jana-gana-mana">Jana Gana Mana</a></li><li><blockquote>On 24 January 1950 the Constituent Assembly, with Rajendra Prasad presiding, declared &quot;<b>Jana Gana Mana</b>&quot; the national anthem and ruled that Vande Mataram, for the part it had played in the struggle for freedom, should be honoured equally with it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/vande-mataram">Vande Mataram</a></li></ul></section>
